{"causes":["The fragmentation of the Kyrgyz tribes due to invasions prompted the need for a unifying narrative.","Cultural influences from neighboring Turkic tribes led to the adaptation and evolution of oral storytelling traditions.","The rise of nationalism in the 19th century inspired a revival of traditional epic poetry as a means of cultural expression."],"effects":["The Epic of Manas has become a symbol of Kyrgyz cultural identity and unity since its formal recognition.","It has influenced the development of Kyrgyz literature and arts, inspiring countless adaptations and performances.","The narrative has been instrumental in fostering national pride and consciousness among the Kyrgyz people."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"kyrgyz-culture","sourceName":"Kyrgyz Culture","verb":"CANONIZES","targetSlug":"epic-of-manas","targetName":"Epic of Manas","context":"The Epic of Manas canonizes the cultural heritage and values of the Kyrgyz people, serving as a foundational text that defines their identity."},{"sourceSlug":"central-asia","sourceName":"Central Asia","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"epic-of-manas","targetName":"Epic of Manas","context":"The diverse cultures and histories of Central Asia influence the themes and narratives found within the Epic of Manas."},{"sourceSlug":"kyrgyzstan","sourceName":"Kyrgyzstan","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"kyrgyz-literature","targetName":"Kyrgyz Literature","context":"The Epic of Manas creates a foundation for the development of Kyrgyz literature, establishing a narrative tradition that persists today."},{"sourceSlug":"epic-of-manas","sourceName":"Epic of Manas","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"manas-hero","targetName":"Manas the Hero","context":"The Epic of Manas contains the legendary figure of Manas, whose heroic deeds symbolize the spirit of the Kyrgyz nation."},{"sourceSlug":"central-asia","sourceName":"Central Asia","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"battles-of-manas","targetName":"Battles of Manas","context":"The narrative of the Epic of Manas occurs in the historical context of Central Asia's battles against foreign invaders."}],"places":[{"name":"Bishkek, Kyrgyzstan","role":"Cultural Center"},{"name":"Osh, Kyrgyzstan","role":"Historical Region"},{"name":"Talas Valley, Kyrgyzstan","role":"Battlefield"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Epic of Manas","type":"Reference article","slug":"epic-of-manas"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q690174","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epic_of_Manas"],"tags":["epic poem","Epic Poetry & Mythology","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Epic of Manas contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Epic of Manas (Kyrgyz: Манас дастаны, Manas dastany) is the national epic of the Kyrgyz people — an enormous cycle of oral heroic poetry narrating the life and deeds of the legendary hero Manas, his son Semetei, and his grandson Seitek (the so-called 'Manas trilogy'), performed by specialist bards called Manaschi (singular: Manaschi). The Manas epic is one of the three great epics of the Mongolian/Central Asian world alongside the Tibetan/Mongolian Epic of King Gesar and the Kalmyk Epic of Jangar, and is of extraordinary length — in its extended versions it is estimated at over 500,000 lines (approximately twenty times the length of Homer's Iliad), though the performed versions by different Manaschi vary enormously in scope. The Manas epic was inscribed on UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2013.

The Manas epic narrates the legendary history of the Kyrgyz people — their migration, their resistance against the Kalmyk (Oirat) invaders, their search for a homeland, and the deeds of the hero Manas as their supreme military and spiritual leader. The epic's core themes — the unity of the Kyrgyz tribes under a heroic leader, the struggle against enemies threatening the survival of the Kyrgyz nation, and the establishment of the Kyrgyz homeland — gave the epic its extraordinary resonance as the primary vehicle of Kyrgyz national identity, particularly after the Kyrgyz conquest by Russia (1855–1876) and during the Soviet period.

The Manaschi tradition — the specialist bards who memorise and perform the Manas epic — is one of the most distinctive features of Kyrgyz oral epic culture. The great 19th-century Manaschi Sagynbay Orozbakovev and Sayakbai Karalaev are considered the most important performers of the tradition, with Karalaev's version (c. 1920–1970) running to approximately 500,000 lines and constituting the most extensive documented version of the epic. The Manas epic was first written down and studied systematically by the Russian explorer-scholar Wilhelm Radloff in 1869, who published extensive excerpts in his work on the Turkic oral tradition.","subjects":["Kyrgyz Literature","Medieval Era","Oral Epic","Central Asian Culture","Nomadic Culture","UNESCO Heritage","Heroic Poetry","Kyrgyzstan"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Epic of Manas significantly shaped Kyrgyz national identity and cultural pride, influencing literature and arts while promoting unity among the Kyrgyz tribes. Its recognition by UNESCO underscores its importance as a cultural heritage that continues to resonate in contemporary Kyrgyz society.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
